body:
A smooth \(n\)-dimensional manifold is a space whose points have neighbourhoods described by \(n\) real coordinates. Where two descriptions overlap, they fit together through smooth changes of coordinates with smooth inverses. This compatibility is what lets us use calculus without committing to one grid. Standard definitions also impose separation and countability conditions (technical restrictions ensuring that points and local descriptions behave well).
The terrain is two-dimensional because a ground point needs two independent local labels. If we draw the surface in three dimensions, height is constrained by the surface: it is not a third independent direction in which we can move while remaining on the ground.
\explanation{local}{Does locally Euclidean mean physically flat?}{It means a neighbourhood has the same smooth coordinate structure as an open region of \(\mathbb R^n\). It does not say that distances measured across that neighbourhood obey Euclidean geometry. Distances, angles and curvature require more structure, such as a metric. A rigid board provides labels by projection; it need not reproduce the ground's distances.}
\explanation{global}{Why not use one grid everywhere?}{A particular projection can cease to be one-to-one or smooth over a larger region. For a whole sphere, for example, no single chart can supply suitable coordinates everywhere. Other regions do admit a single chart, even if curved. So curvature alone does not prohibit global coordinates. The scientists use overlapping boards because those local projections are a convenient way to organise their survey.}

A coordinate chart on an \(n\)-dimensional \cref{manifold}{math.manifold.definition} \(M\) is a map \(x:U\to x(U)\subset\mathbb R^n\). Here \(U\) is an open region of the manifold and \(x(U)\) is open in \(\mathbb R^n\). Each point receives a unique coordinate tuple, and the inverse map takes that tuple back to its point. So the same chart cannot give two different ground points the same labels. Smoothness is understood relative to the manifold's compatible charts, so coordinate calculations can be transferred between them.
An atlas is a collection of compatible charts covering the manifold. A point in an overlap can have one tuple from each chart. The tuples differ, but each identifies the same point.
\explanation{inverse}{Why require an inverse?}{If two ground points project to the same board position, the labels cannot tell them apart. If an inverse breaks down, calculations in those labels may cease to describe the surface smoothly. We must then shrink or replace the patch: the coordinate failure does not mean the ground point itself has disappeared.}

A peg in an overlap has coordinates \(x^1,x^2\) in one scientist's records and \(y^1,y^2\) in another's. Their agreed rule \(y=y(x)\) translates between those records, identifying the same peg in both.
Although each board has a straight grid, the conversion need not be linear. The boards project curved ground onto differently positioned tangent planes, so a simple movement across one grid need not correspond to a constant-rate movement across the other. Differences in scale, orientation and obliqueness also enter the conversion.
\explanation{affine}{When is a simple linear rule enough?}{For two straight grids on one flat plane, the rule is affine: \(y^i=A^i{}_j x^j+b^i\), where \(A\) is an invertible constant matrix. The matrix changes scales and directions, while \(b^i\) shifts the origin. This useful special case should not be assumed for neighbouring projections of curved terrain.}

On the two-dimensional terrain use \(i,j=1,2\). In four-dimensional spacetime this atlas usually uses Greek indices \(\mu,\nu=0,1,2,3\). Before expanding any sum, check the range: it tells us how many terms to include.
A four-vector therefore has four components, while a two-index spacetime array has 16 entries before symmetries. Thus the range records dimension, while the number and positions of the slots record tensor type. These two counts play different roles, as in the \cref{type and order examples}{math.tensor_field.rank}.
\explanation{power}{A component label versus a power}{In two dimensions, \(V^i=(3,4)\) means \(V^1=3\) and \(V^2=4\). The square of the second component is \((V^2)^2=16\). The outer superscript squares a number; the inner one labels the chosen component. Parentheses help keep those roles separate.}

Under Einstein summation, a label repeated once upstairs and once downstairs in a term tells us to sum over its allowed values. In two dimensions,
\[A^i B_i=A^1B_1+A^2B_2.\]
For \(A^i=(2,-1)\) and \(B_i=(3,4)\), this is \(2(3)+(-1)(4)=2\). Since every allowed value is used in the sum, the name \(i\) only keeps track of the pairing. It is a dummy label: replacing both copies by \(j\) changes no term or answer.
\explanation{free}{What is a free index?}{A free index is not summed; it appears once in each term. The equation \(C^i=A^i+B^i\) represents one equation for every allowed \(i\). In two dimensions it means \(C^1=A^1+B^1\) and \(C^2=A^2+B^2\). We need the same free labels and positions in every term on both sides, because each term must refer to the same component equation.}
\explanation{check}{Spot an invalid equation}{In \(T^{\mu\nu}=V^\mu\), the free index \(\nu\) appears only on the left, so there is no matching right-hand component for that label, so the equation is incomplete. In contrast \(T^{\mu\nu}=V^\mu W^\nu\) has matching free indices. A dummy index should occur exactly twice in a term, once up and once down. Using the same label three times in a term makes the intended pairing ambiguous, so it does not define an Einstein sum.}

To identify a tensor's type, count its upper and lower index slots. A tensor of type \((r,s)\) has \(r\) upper and \(s\) lower indices; its total order, also called total rank here, is \(r+s\).
A scalar has type \((0,0)\) and order zero. A vector \(V^\mu\) has type \((1,0)\); a covector \(\omega_\mu\) has type \((0,1)\). Both have one slot, so both have order one. Their types differ because they accept different inputs and transform differently. A metric \(g_{\mu
u}\) has type \((0,2)\) and order two. A mixed tensor \(A^\mu{}_{
u}\) has type \((1,1)\) and order two.
\explanation{count}{How many component entries are there?}{In \(n\) dimensions each index takes \(n\) values. There are \(n\) choices for each of \(k\) indices, so the full component array has \(n^k\) entries before we use symmetries or constraints. A four-dimensional metric has \(4^2=16\) entries. Symmetry \(g_{\mu
u}=g_{
u\mu}\) leaves 4 diagonal and 6 off-diagonal values, hence 10 independent components.}
\explanation{matrix-rank}{Is this the rank of a matrix?}{No. Matrix rank counts independent rows or columns of a linear map's array. Tensor order counts index slots. For example, a zero component matrix can represent an order-two tensor while having matrix rank zero. These are two different meanings of rank. Also, a tensor of order four can live on a two-dimensional manifold: having four slots does not require four independent coordinate directions. Tensor order and manifold dimension answer different questions.}
